```js
router.get('/api/invoices/:id', requireAuth, async (req, res) => {
  const invoice = await Invoice.findById(req.params.id);
  res.json(invoice);
});
```

Logged in as yourself, `/api/invoices/4471` returns your invoice. Change the number in the address bar to `/api/invoices/4470` and it returns somebody else's — full amount, billing address, everything — because `Invoice.findById` looks a record up by its own id and nothing else, and `requireAuth` only confirmed you're logged in as *a* user, not that you're the user this particular invoice belongs to. This has a name — Broken Object-Level Authorization (BOLA), also called IDOR, insecure direct object reference — and it's reliably one of the most common vulnerabilities found in real API security audits, because "look this record up by the id in the URL" is the single most natural way to write a get-by-id handler, and scoping it to the caller is an extra clause nobody's shorthand request mentioned.

