A generated retry wrapper:

```python
def call_with_retry(fn, max_attempts=10):
    for attempt in range(max_attempts):
        try:
            return fn()
        except requests.RequestException:
            continue
    raise Exception("All retries failed")
```

A downstream service starts struggling under load — not fully down, just slow, timing out on maybe half its requests. Every caller using this wrapper immediately retries a failed call, with no delay between attempts, up to ten times. What was a service handling requests slowly is now receiving up to ten times its normal request volume from every caller that just got a timeout, instantly, with zero pause — which pushes a struggling service the rest of the way into fully down, and keeps it there, because the flood of immediate retries never lets up long enough for it to catch up on its existing backlog.
